from ./ExpansionRules import ExpansionRules
from ../meaning/Strengths import Strengths
from ../meaning/Facts import Facts
from ../model/OpenItems import OpenItems
from ../model import SemanticModel

export anchor Instructions:
    description:
        The same semantic model that measures a specification can also be
        compiled into instructions for an implementation agent: a document
        per entity that says, as plainly and redundantly as it takes, what
        must be built, what follows from it, and what has not been decided.

        The model is the source. The instructions are only a way of writing
        it down, the way JSON is a way of writing down compiled Piton. Their
        goal is not to read well. Their goal is that an agent following them
        has nothing left to interpret.

    theRule:
        Instructions never say more than the model. Every sentence in them
        comes from a fact or an open item in the @{SemanticModel}, through a
        fixed template, and cites where it came from. A sentence may repeat a
        requirement another way, but only through the rules in
        @{ExpansionRules}, and never in a way that adds to it.

        Nothing is dropped either. Every requirement appears, and every open
        item appears, so an agent can tell the difference between something
        the specification did not mention and something it left undecided.

    fourKindsOfLine:
        ```text
        requirement         must be done, because the specification says so
        derived requirement must be done, because it follows from what the
                            specification says; the rule is named
        open decision       has not been decided, and must not be decided by
                            the implementer
        prohibition         what the implementer must not assume, for each
                            open decision
        ```

        Requirements come from facts that are not partial, restricted,
        contested or uncommitted. Everything else appears only as an open
        decision, described in @{OpenItems}.

    strengthWords:
        Each requirement is written with the word its strength renders as, in
        capitals, as listed in @{Strengths}: MUST, SHOULD, or MAY. A negated
        requirement uses MUST NOT, SHOULD NOT or MAY NOT.

    templates:
        Each fact form has one template. Words in angle brackets are filled
        in from the fact.

        ```text
        value            <subject>.<aspect> MUST be <value>.
        transformation   <subject>.<aspect> MUST be the result of <verb> applied
                         to <argument>.
        choice           <subject>.<aspect> MUST have exactly one of these values
                         at any time: <values>.
        capability       <subject> MUST be <able form>.
                         or, with no able form, <subject> MUST be able to <verb>,
                         or MUST be able to be <past participle>.
        trigger          When <subject> is <past participle>, <target> MUST be
                         invoked.
        relation         <subject> MUST <verb> <target>.
        ```

        Facts are described in @{Facts}.

    documentShape:
        An instruction document has four parts, always in this order.

        ```text
        heading        the entity's name, and where it is declared
        always         requirements with no conditions
        while ...      one section per set of conditions, in the order the
                       conditions first appear in the source
        open decisions every open item about the entity, each followed by
                       its prohibition
        ```

        Within a section, lines are ordered by the source position of the
        fact they come from. Every line ends with its citation: stated or
        derived, the rule for a derived line, the statement, and the file and
        line.

    prohibitions:
        Each open decision is followed by two fixed sentences. The first is
        the forTheImplementer sentence of its kind. The second is always the
        same template.

        ```text
        Do not invent <what is open>. If the implementation cannot proceed
        without it, stop and report open decision <number>.
        ```

    example:
        The running example, compiled into instructions.

        ```markdown
        # Button

        Declared in button.pi, line 1.

        ## Always

        1. Button.state MUST have exactly one of these values at any time:
           enabled, disabled. It MUST NOT have any other value.
           (stated; Button.description/s4; button.pi:6)
        2. Button.color MUST be blue.
           (stated; Button.description/s3; button.pi:6)

        ## While Button.state is enabled

        3. While Button.state is enabled, Button MUST be clickable.
           (derived by SpecificOverGeneral and ChoiceComplement from
           Button.description/s1 and Button.disabled/s3)
        4. While Button.state is enabled, when Button is clicked,
           Button.action MUST be invoked.
           (derived by SpecificOverGeneral and ChoiceComplement from
           Button.description/s2 and requirement 8)

        ## While Button.state is disabled

        5. While Button.state is disabled, Button.color MUST be the result of
           dim applied to Button.color. See open decision 2.
           (stated; Button.disabled/s1; button.pi:9)
        6. While Button.state is disabled, Button.text MUST be the result of
           dim applied to Button.text. See open decisions 2 and 3.
           (stated; Button.disabled/s2; button.pi:9)
        7. While Button.state is disabled, Button MUST NOT be clickable. A
           click on Button MUST NOT be accepted.
           (stated; Button.disabled/s3; button.pi:9)
        8. While Button.state is disabled, when Button is clicked,
           Button.action MUST NOT be invoked.
           (derived by SuppressedEffect from Button.description/s2 and
           Button.disabled/s3)

        ## Open decisions

        1. Button.action is used but never defined.
           (gap; introduced-part; Button.description/s2; button.pi:3)
           Do not fill this in. It has not been decided.
           Do not invent what Button.action is. If the implementation cannot
           proceed without it, stop and report open decision 1.
        2. dim is applied to Button.color and Button.text but never defined.
           (gap; undefined-transformation; Button.disabled/s1, s2; button.pi:9)
           Do not fill this in. It has not been decided.
           Do not invent what dim does. If the implementation cannot proceed
           without it, stop and report open decision 2.
        3. Button.text is used but never defined.
           (gap; introduced-part; Button.disabled/s2; button.pi:9)
           Do not fill this in. It has not been decided.
           Do not invent what Button.text is. If the implementation cannot
           proceed without it, stop and report open decision 3.
        ```

        Line 7 says the same requirement twice, the second time through
        CapabilityAsInput, and line 1 does the same through
        ChoiceExclusivity. Lines 3, 4 and 8 were never written by the author,
        and say so.

    withBelay:
        Belay, the framework bundled with Piton, already turns Piton into the
        documents agents read, and its instruction keyword places guidance
        beside the code it applies to. An instruction document from analysis is
        the explicit form of such guidance: for an entity declared as a Belay
        instruction, it is what the prose of that instruction commits the
        implementer to.

        This section defines the instruction document and nothing about where
        it is written. Belay's own specification is not part of this
        repository, so whether Belay's build writes instruction documents
        beside the guidance it already produces is left to that
        specification. Until then, instruction documents are produced by the
        analyze command.
